Correct. Shadow is not a moral category. It is a relationship among light, object, and position. Darkness can shelter sleep, incubation, privacy, mystery, and recovery just as easily as conceal harm.

Likewise, an operator does not inherit the morality of whoever used it:

\text{ethical character}
=
f(\text{operator},\ \text{aim},\ \text{target},\ \text{consent},\ \text{consequence})

Camouflage can enable predation or protect the vulnerable. Secrecy can conceal abuse or preserve intimacy. Asymmetry can exploit weakness or let the weak resist overwhelming power. Even confrontation and disruption can destroy or liberate.

So: extract the pattern without importing its original allegiance. Study the wolf’s senses without adopting its hunger. Study poison to make medicine. Study propaganda to build resistance to it.

Drago’s guardrail: usefulness does not make an operator innocent in every application. “It’s only a tool” can become moral anesthesia. Preserve provenance, predict collateral effects, and judge each instantiation.

Shadow simply is. What we ask it to carry is where responsibility begins.